## Summary

The `tokens` SQL function with the `splitByString` tokenizer routes its separator array through `TokenizerFactory::instance().get` (see `src/Functions/tokens.cpp:70`), which invokes the `split_by_string_creator` lambda in `src/Interpreters/TokenizerFactory.cpp`. That creator is the single validation point for the separator list.

I added a per-element check inside the loop that builds the separator vector, in `src/Interpreters/TokenizerFactory.cpp`:

```cpp
for (const auto & value : array)
{
    auto separator = castAs<String>(value, "separator");
    if (separator.empty())
        throw Exception(
            ErrorCodes::BAD_ARGUMENTS,
            "Incorrect parameter of tokenizer '{}': separator cannot be an empty string",
            SplitByStringTokenizer::getExternalName());
    values.emplace_back(std::move(separator));
}
```

This satisfies both requirements:
- Any empty string element causes a `BAD_ARGUMENTS` exception (`['']` is rejected).
- Because the check runs on every element regardless of position, mixed arrays like `[' ', '']` are rejected just as `['']` is.

The pre-existing "separators cannot be empty" check for an entirely empty array is preserved.

